@*
    OdsSecretClearOnSaveDialog — the gate in front of a page SAVE that clears a stored secret as a
    side effect of changing something else (Odyssey Design System · components/SecretClearOnSaveDialog).

    DISTINCT FROM OdsSecretClearDialog, which confirms an immediate, single-field clear the user asked
    for directly. Here the administrator asked to change a DIFFERENT value — the SMTP host, the
    STARTTLS flag — and the clear is a consequence of that change, committed in the same transaction.

    TWO COPY VARIANTS, chosen by Reason, because the two triggers protect against different things:

      Host      a credential entered for one relay must never be presented to another — the SMTP
                client connects first and authenticates second, so the new host would receive it.
                A PORT change uses this variant too: host and port together are the endpoint, and a
                different listener on the same host receives the credential just as readily.
      StartTls  a credential entered for an encrypted transport must never be replayed over a
                cleartext one, where passive network position alone is enough to harvest it.

    THREE THINGS THE COPY MUST SAY, and no dialog above it can: what is cleared, that the clear and
    the save commit together or not at all, and — because this gates a WHOLE-PAGE batch save — that
    Cancel discards nothing. That last one is why the confirm button counts the pending edits it is
    about to submit: this is the page's single Save, not a per-field one, and a reader who has edited
    six rows needs to know all six are going.

    The design system is explicit that OdsSecretClearDialog's COMPONENT shape is the right precedent
    to reuse and its trigger and scope are not: that dialog fires from a field's own Clear button,
    this one intercepts the page's Save.

    Focus restoration is the CALLER's job, not this component's — neither OdsModal nor OdsFormDialog
    restores it, and only the caller knows which control opened the dialog. Settings.razor.cs returns
    focus to the triggering row on Confirm, Cancel and Escape alike.
*@

<OdsFormDialog Open="Open"
               OpenChanged="OpenChanged"
               Icon="@(IsStartTls ? "lock_open" : "key_off")"
               IconTone="OdsModalTone.Error"
               Title="@TitleText"
               CancelLabel="Cancel"
               SubmitLabel="@ConfirmText"
               SubmitIcon="save"
               SubmitVariant="OdsButtonVariant.Danger"
               OnSubmit="OnConfirm">

    @if (IsStartTls)
    {
        <p class="odc-clearsave-p">
            The connection to
            @if (Named(FromHost) is { } from)
            {
                <b>@from</b>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>the relay</text>
            }
            will no longer be encrypted. Anyone in a position to watch that traffic can read the
            credential and every message sent over it, including password-reset links.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="odc-clearsave-p">
            Mail will be relayed through
            @if (Named(ToHost) is { } to)
            {
                <b>@to</b>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>the new host</text>
            }
            @if (Named(FromHost) is { } from)
            {
                <text> instead of </text><b>@from</b>
            }
            . The SMTP client connects first and authenticates second, so whatever host is set here
            receives the stored credential.
        </p>
    }

    <p class="odc-clearsave-p">
        <b>@SecretList</b> @(Secrets.Count == 1 ? "is" : "are") cleared by this save, in the same
        transaction — either both the change and the clear land, or neither does. Re-enter the
        credential at <b>@ReEnterAt</b> afterwards; until you do, transactional mail is sent
        unauthenticated and any relay that requires a login will reject it.
    </p>

    @* The whole-page-save clause. Not a nicety: without it a reader who has edited six rows has no
       way to know whether Cancel keeps or discards the other five, and the safe assumption (discard)
       is the wrong one. *@
    <p class="odc-clearsave-p">
        <b>Cancel discards nothing.</b> Every edit on this page stays exactly as you left it and
        nothing is saved, so you can put this one value back by hand and save the rest.
    </p>
</OdsFormDialog>

@code {
    /// <summary>Controls visibility. Bindable via @@bind-Open.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Open { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> OpenChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which change triggered the clear, and therefore which copy variant is used.</summary>
    [Parameter] public OdsCredentialClearReason Reason { get; set; } = OdsCredentialClearReason.Host;

    /// <summary>
    /// The relay being moved away from, or the one losing encryption. Empty when unconfigured.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// It carries an ENDPOINT, not always a bare host: when the SMTP port is what changed, the caller
    /// appends it (<c>smtp.example.net:465</c>), because a port-only change is the same threat as a
    /// host change — a different listener the credential is handed to — and naming the same host on
    /// both sides of "instead of" would read as a bug in this dialog. The parameter keeps the design
    /// system's name so the contract still matches <c>SecretClearOnSaveDialog</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? FromHost { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The relay being moved to, on the same terms. Host variant only.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? ToHost { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Titles of the secrets this save clears, e.g. "SMTP username" and "SMTP password".</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<string> Secrets { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Where the credential is re-entered afterwards.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string ReEnterAt { get; set; } = "Credentials";

    /// <summary>
    /// Pending edits on the page, named on the confirm button. This gates a whole-page batch save, so
    /// the count is what tells the reader how much Confirm is about to submit.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int PendingCount { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Returns <c>true</c> to close. Returning <c>false</c> keeps the dialog open on failure.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<Task<bool>>? OnConfirm { get; set; }

    private bool IsStartTls => Reason == OdsCredentialClearReason.StartTls;

    /// <summary>
    /// The dialog title, pinned by a test alongside the announcement. It leads with the CONSEQUENCE
    /// rather than the change, because the change is the thing the administrator already knows they
    /// asked for and the consequence is the thing they did not.
    /// </summary>
    internal string TitleText => IsStartTls
        ? "Turning STARTTLS off clears the stored SMTP credential"
        : "Changing the SMTP host clears the stored SMTP credential";

    /// <summary>
    /// The confirm label. "Save 3 changes and clear" says something "Confirm" does not, and this is
    /// the last thing a reader sees before a destructive whole-page save.
    /// </summary>
    internal string ConfirmText => PendingCount > 0
        ? $"Save {PendingCount} change{(PendingCount == 1 ? string.Empty : "s")} and clear"
        : "Save and clear";

    /// <summary>
    /// The secrets, listed as prose rather than as bullets. Two items at most in practice, and a
    /// two-item bulleted list reads as a longer consequence than it is.
    /// </summary>
    private string SecretList => Secrets.Count switch
    {
        0 => "The stored SMTP credential",
        1 => Secrets[0],
        _ => string.Join(", ", Secrets.Take(Secrets.Count - 1)) + " and " + Secrets[^1],
    };

    private static string? Named(string? host) => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(host) ? null : host;
}
